## How to Make Your Teacher Nails Last All Week

Wednesday afternoon. Year 5 have just finished their art project. You have distributed, collected, washed brushes, wiped three tables, and your nails  done Sunday evening  are already lifting at the corner of the index finger.

That is the moment no competitor post prepares you for. <strong>Back to school nails for teachers</strong> need to survive real classroom conditions, not just look good in the photo.

Gel polish is the minimum baseline for teacher durability. Standard gel gives you 10–14 days without chipping under normal classroom conditions, provided prep is thorough. Prep is the word. Pushing back the cuticle properly, lightly buffing the nail plate, applying a strong base coat to the edges  this is what determines whether your nails survive a Tuesday afternoon with Year 3 and still look intentional on Wednesday morning. Skipping prep to save five minutes costs you three days of wear.

BIAB (Builder in a Bottle) is the teacher's strongest formula option. BIAB is a structured gel overlay that adds physical strength to the natural nail without extending length, lasting up to three weeks with minimal maintenance. It resists the impact, board-wiping friction, and constant handwashing that standard gel starts to lose to by week two. The overlay is thin enough that nail art sits cleanly on top.

Dip powder is the alternative for teachers who prefer a salon visit over home gel application  it cures without UV light, tends to be highly durable, and comes in every professional neutral and school-appropriate colour imaginable.

Whichever formula you choose, a quality top coat reapplied at day five or six adds 3–4 additional days of wear. A two-minute Sunday evening touch-up is the cheapest durability extension available.

